<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso de Molina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1202–<a href="/years/1272">1272</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso of León, Lord of Molina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso de Molina was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a>. Through his marriage to the heiress Mafalda he became lord of Molina, tying the semi-independent lordship to the Castilian royal family. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-de-molina">María de Molina</a> became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the lordship later passed to the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-de-molina.jpg" alt="Alfonso de Molina" width="172" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alonso de Cartagena · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso_de_Molina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfonso was born near León in 1202, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> and his second wife, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berengaria of Castile</a>, and so a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After his parents' marriage was annulled he grew up amid bad relations between the two kingdoms. In 1222 his brother Ferdinand III quarrelled with Gonzalo Pérez de Lara, Lord of Molina, a supporter of Alfonso IX, and besieged him in the Castle of Zafra. Under the Treaty of Zafra the lordship was to pass not to Gonzalo's son but to his daughter Mafalda, who would marry the infante Alfonso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The marriage took place in 1240, and on Gonzalo's death in 1243 Alfonso became Lord of Molina, governing it for the rest of his life and expanding the provisions of its fueros. In 1230, when Alfonso IX named his daughters Sancha and Dulce as his heirs in León, Alfonso had refused the throne, and Ferdinand rewarded him with lands and privileges. Various historians hold that it was he, not the king's young son, who took part in the raid that ended in the Castilian victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud">Ibn Hud</a> at the Battle of Jerez in 1231.</p><p>He distinguished himself in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> in 1236, and at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1248 he captured the Torre de Oro and occupied part of the Álcazar of Seville, known as the Walls of the Infante of Molina. In the division of the territory of Seville on 1 May 1253, under his nephew Alfonso X, he received large grants as the late king's younger brother. In 1254 he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>, and he attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of Valladolid in 1258 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1259.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfonso died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> on 6 January 1272, aged 70, and his remains were later moved from the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> there to Calatrava la Nueva, as his will asked; neither tomb survives. He married three times: Mafalda, then Teresa González de Lara in 1244 and Mayor Alfonso de Meneses in 1260. His daughter Blanca inherited the Lordship of Molina, and his daughter by his third marriage, Maria of Molina, married her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-castile">Sancho IV of Castile</a> and was the mother of King Ferdinand IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso of Molina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 13th century the heiress Mafalda married the infante <b>Alfonso de Molina</b>, brother of Ferdinand III, tying it closer to the Castilian royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-molina">Lordship of Molina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in León, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=1247" aria-label="León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfonso de Molina"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4650 3923h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4650 3923h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.6,-5.57&amp;year=1247">What was here in 1247? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
